# 0002. Conventional Commits, squash merges, automated releases

- Status: accepted
- Date: @@today@@

## Context

Changes arrive through pull requests from people and coding agents. @@#if release_please@@Versions and changelogs should follow from the
history without manual work.@@/if@@@@#unless release_please@@The history should be readable and searchable by type and area.@@/unless@@

## Decision

Commit messages and pull request titles follow Conventional Commits with a fixed type list, enforced by a commit-msg hook,
a script in CI and a pull request title check. Pull requests are squash-merged, so the title becomes the commit on
`@@default_branch@@`. @@#if release_please@@release-please reads `@@default_branch@@` and maintains a release pull request with the version bump and the
changelog; merging it tags the release. @@/if@@`@@default_branch@@` is protected: a pull request and green required checks, linear history, no force
push.

## Consequences

@@#if release_please@@A release costs one merge. @@/if@@A non-conventional title or message is rejected early. Contributors must learn the convention;
`CONTRIBUTING.md` and `AGENTS.md` document it. The hooks are tool-neutral shell scripts, so they behave the same for every
coding agent and editor.

## Alternatives considered

Manual tagging and changelog: forgotten in practice. Merge commits: noisier history, and release automation reads branch
commits inconsistently. A commit linter that needs a language runtime: not available in every stack of the repository.
